            {
                "entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                "word": "idolopée",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "d2ddb1bf-292e-4ccd-908c-5c94d966547a",
                        "entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "idolopées"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                        "entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Figure de rhétorique, qui a lieu lorsqu’on fait parler une personne morte."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "rhetoric"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "758620e8-0758-40d1-9c30-fb3a2b60dd7b",
                                "sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                                "text": "Les Grecs appelloient en général Ethopée l’art d’imiter les mœurs. Cet art se divisoit en trois parties : L’Ethopée proprement dite, ou la peinture des moeurs & habitudes d’une personne connue ; l’Idolopée où l’on introduisoit sur la scène un personnage mort ; & enfin la Prosopée, où tout étoit feint, jusqu’au personnage."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5621f406-8033-458f-b839-311a0b2a1827",
                        "source_entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "source_sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                        "target_lang_word": "eidolopoeia",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "eidolopoeia",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "91475188-7e8d-345c-ab38-3e34f7644724",
                                        "entry_id": "25c0c612-7fb3-3109-81b9-783f178e3084",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A rhetorical technique in which a speech is attributed to a deceased person, a phantom, an image or an idol."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rhetoric",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3349bd12-2304-41f7-8daf-6b4e398e9077",
                                                "sense_id": "91475188-7e8d-345c-ab38-3e34f7644724",
                                                "text": "The status of the speaker at the time the speech is imagined as being given is what determines whether it is ethopoeia or eidolopoeia. A speech Heracles might have given while alive is an example of ethopoeia, a speech he might have given after death is an eidolopoeia"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "748733f7-bac1-4649-9e2b-fd586ac03e6b",
                        "source_entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "source_sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                        "target_lang_word": "eidolopeia",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "eidolopeia",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "225f456a-9495-3ec0-a046-7b90459b1a8f",
                                        "entry_id": "0424d4d4-fe5d-3305-9731-b97f98904b40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of eidolopoeia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b4a3233e-18ad-4a16-9f85-405854273ea5",
                                                "sense_id": "225f456a-9495-3ec0-a046-7b90459b1a8f",
                                                "text": "Composing speech and placing it in the mouth of a known living person, thereby inventing that person's ethos, is referred to as ethopoeia; the similar in the mouth of a dead person is eidolopeia; and when both the ethos and actual person are invented, it is known as prosopoiia"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "19224b8a-8c66-40a8-90d2-10769a9a854c",
                        "source_entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "source_sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                        "target_lang_word": "idolopoeia",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "idolopoeia",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7e470c25-881b-3076-956a-0b3594a0a02d",
                                        "entry_id": "240ebd36-350c-37b8-a1ec-12da1f18a365",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of eidolopoeia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "26307b78-9f18-43e5-9033-bd2684bfc606",
                                                "sense_id": "7e470c25-881b-3076-956a-0b3594a0a02d",
                                                "text": "The plays' capacity to present the political actors on stage as characters actively engaged in interpreting and thus making their way through the very plots that their own actions and interactions were constituting owed a great deal to forensic rhetoric and the training in theopoeia (impersonating historical and mythological characters), prosopoeia (impersonating abstractions or 'things unknown'), and idolopoeia (impersonating dead people)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4c908b0c-1dfe-4de1-9783-5343a3587cf5",
                                                "sense_id": "7e470c25-881b-3076-956a-0b3594a0a02d",
                                                "text": "Idolopoeia or ghost-making (as a figure of style) is that which represents a famous person who is really dead and no longer able to speak"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c1931332-f794-4663-bd89-1f3c290c9ef2",
                        "source_entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "source_sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                        "target_lang_word": "idolopeia",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f110ea05-5b1a-4d14-9baf-c0cfd12d55fb",
                        "source_entry_id": "60023516-4f13-370f-a851-f209e5f93aef",
                        "source_sense_id": "877fec34-7a75-31aa-ad38-c0698b7c586f",
                        "target_lang_word": "idolopeya",
                        "target_lang_code": "es",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "idolopeya",
                                "target_lang_code": "es",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4f8dc1b7-0195-3e45-a3dd-eca53751154d",
                                        "entry_id": "9079e344-5ad0-3106-952b-fab3e06b25c2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Figura retórica o literaria en la cual se atribuye a un difunto, a un fantasma, a una imagen, o a un ídolo, algún discurso, que puede ser elegiaco o de cualquier otra índole."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Retórica"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7d1ca960-181f-4f6b-a5a3-eb1d9ae55d55",
                                                "sense_id": "4f8dc1b7-0195-3e45-a3dd-eca53751154d",
                                                "text": "Padecía de un mal que tiene su denominación retórica, como achaque de poetas y de los héroes trágicos y épicos, y consiste en la presencia de personajes imaginarios que hablan, sombras de entes que han existido, y que vuelven a este mundo a manifestar algo de interés para los vivos. A tal forma de personificación llaman los eruditos idolopeya"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "474c0e04-d828-4234-b094-f80d504dbdfb",
                                                "sense_id": "4f8dc1b7-0195-3e45-a3dd-eca53751154d",
                                                "text": "Los griegos denominaban etopeya al arte de imitar las costumbres [de una persona]. Este arte se dividía en tres partes: La etopeya propiamente dicha, o el retrato de los hábitos de una persona conocida; la idolopeya, en la cual se introducía en la escena a una persona muerto; y la prosopeya, en la que todo era falso, incluyendo la persona."
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "beed6640-97da-4707-a850-d5f8c57c31a3",
